 of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Fresno has set <strong class=\"sig\">1 standing record low</strong>, far fewer than the 70 that chance alone would give, and <strong class=\"sig\">117 record highs</strong>, far more than the 70 that chance alone would give. The 2020s hold the most record highs of any decade (47). Fresno's all-time high is 115\u00b0F, on July 8, 1905.","t_chg":"Fresno has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 138 complete years: Fresno's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.27\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.20 to +0.34), about +3.7\u00b0F since 1888. Fresno's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.15\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.10 to +0.20), about +2.1\u00b0F since 1888.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 10 of 12 months. Highs rose in 7 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Fresno's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.10 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 11.0 inches a year. The wettest year was 1983 (21.61 in); the driest, 2013 (3.01 in). Fresno almost never sees snow (the 1991\u20132020 average is 0.0 inches a winter), so there's no snowfall chart.","show_snow":false,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":true},"t_year":"2026 so far: 11 daily record highs, no daily record lows"}}